<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albrecht von Haller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1708–1777</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albrecht von Haller was a Swiss physiologist, anatomist, botanist and poet from Bern, often called the father of modern physiology for his experiments on the irritability of muscles and the sensibility of nerves. He taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> from 1736 to 1753. His poem Die Alpen of 1729 helped create the image of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and their people as simple and virtuous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-haller.jpg" alt="Albrecht von Haller" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Rudolf Huber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_von_Haller_1736.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
